COMMIT 19ee0fa162734c0a5490f18ebbde3e3421cc2305
test(core): FIX-131 pin the stitched timeline a redelivered message leaves

Two cases in a new RedeliveredTimelineTest, both green on today's code, both
documenting a defect so CONSIDER-12 has a regression test to invert.

The row asked for an -Pit test against a real Postgres before anything was
designed. I withdrew that premise: both stores drop a repeated (id, step) by
construction -- `on conflict (id, step) do nothing` in PgMessageStore and
`rows.putIfAbsent` in InMemoryMessageStore -- so a container proves nothing a
core test does not, and the Postgres half is already pinned by
PgMessageStoreIT:122-132 under a name ending DuplicatesAreIgnored. That name is
fair for an idempotent batch replay and wrong for a second run of one message,
and it is part of why this went unnoticed.

The harm is also worse than the row described, and is now measured rather than
argued. Case 1, with the park refused so run 1 leaves a FAILED row: timeline(id)
returns 0 ingress, steps 1-2 from run 1, step 3 FAILED from run 1, steps 4-7 OK
from run 2 -- a FAILED step followed by successful later steps, which no single
run can produce. Case 2 is the shape a node crash makes, and it is the dangerous
one: run 1 dies on an Error, which Recorder.wrap does not catch, so step 3 gets
no row at all, and the timeline comes back as steps 0-7 all OK, indistinguishable
from one clean successful run. The first shape invites an operator's question;
the second does not.

One correction to my own description, from the measurement: the seam is not
unmarked. Envelope.ATTEMPT is in the props of every row run 2 wrote and in none
of run 1's. It does not rescue the timeline -- a props entry is not a run
boundary, and it is a property of the rows that survived rather than a statement
about the rows that did not -- but it does mean D-108 has the value it needs
already reaching the store. What is missing is a column that means it.

Evidence: VERIFIED_PASS, 2/0 for the new class and 42/0 for FlowWorkerTest, 46/0
in total (agent/work/FIX-131-test4.log). Hand mutant `putIfAbsent` -> `put` in
InMemoryMessageStore: both cases EXECUTED_FAIL at their final exact-list
assertion (FIX-131-mutant.log), so they are green because the store keeps the
first run's row on a collision and not for some other reason. The store was
restored with `cp` and verified byte-identical to HEAD by `cmp` in both trees, by
me rather than on the author's word; no production file is changed here.

The review's one FAIL is worth recording. Case 2 pins FIX-133 as well as the key
defect, and its javadoc named only CONSIDER-12 as a reason the test would ever be
inverted -- so an implementer of FIX-133, which does not wait for CONSIDER-12,
would have met red tests it is not allowed to change. The javadoc now names both
triggers and every assertion each one flips. Spun off FIX-133 (Recorder.wrap
catches Exception only, so a step that throws an Error leaves no row at all --
the same asymmetry D-104 already settled for BatchingWriter) and FIX-132.

